      "type": "done",
      "answer": "The currently imported lecture does not contain any information about a user named \"yuxinye,\" their specific TACC job, or any real-time GPU utilization metrics for that job.\n\nThe lecture material provided discusses general concepts of memory walls, GPU memory hierarchies, and capacity walls in the context of AI computing systems [S1][S2][S3]. Specifically, page 65 mentions that \"GPU is much underutilized!\" in the context of a capacity wall where HBM can only hold limited KV-Caches, resulting in low batch size and computation utilization [S1]. However, this is a general observation about system limitations, not a specific real-time metric for a particular user's job.\n\nTherefore, it is not possible to provide the real-time GPU utilization of yuxinye's current TACC job using only the currently imported lecture.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p65-2",
          "page": 65,
          "text": "M          ~45.75 GiB          ~4.83 GiB\n                                 GPU is much underutilized!\n     2M          ~91.50 GiB          ~9.65 GiB\n\n\n  Capacity Wall: HBM can only hold limited KV-Caches, resulting in low\n  batch size and computation utilization.\n\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory     GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        65",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/65/preview",
          "source": "S1"
        },
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p40-1",
          "page": 40,
          "text": "CPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nDRAM Behind Cache: Channel Bandwidth Grows Along Three\nAxes\n\n\n\n\n\n  Independent channels add bandwidth when requests use them concurrently. Bank\n▪\n  scheduling improves bus utilization.\n DDR5 splits 64 data bits into two 32-bit subchannels. This split alone does not\n▪\n  double total data width.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        40",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/40/preview",
          "source": "S2"
        },
        {
          "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
          "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p47-1",
          "page": 47,
          "text": "GPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nAccess Memory: One Warp Memory Instruction Generates Up to\n32 Addresses\n\n\n\n\n\n▪ The coalescer groups addresses by cache-sector or transaction granularity before\n DRAM access.\n▪ The transaction count depends on alignment, address spacing, access width, and\n GPU architecture.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        47",
          "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/47/preview",
